event: response.function_call_arguments.done
data: {"type":"response.function_call_arguments.done","sequence_number":481,"item_id":"fc_04de6b92baab60ea016adb473600b48191b5dc6c3125f68895","output_index":1,"arguments":"{\"artifact_json\":\"{\\\"base_candidate\\\":41,\\\"patches\\\":[{\\\"op\\\":\\\"replace\\\",\\\"path\\\":\\\"/graph/inferences/3/locals/4/from_inputs\\\",\\\"value\\\":[\\\"M019\\\"]},{\\\"op\\\":\\\"replace\\\",\\\"path\\\":\\\"/graph/inferences/3/locals/4/from_local\\\",\\\"value\\\":[]},{\\\"op\\\":\\\"replace\\\",\\\"path\\\":\\\"/graph/inferences/3/argument/0/text\\\",\\\"value\\\":\\\"Fix arbitrary r>0, let J=[r/2,3r/2], choose compact C from H003, and define C*=C∪{0}. The set C* is compact and nonempty; outside C* we still have u=q throughout J. The function (x,τ)↦|e_t(x,τ)| is continuous on C*×J by smoothness, and C*×J is compact by M005. Apply M019; its bounded-above conclusion supplies a real upper bound, so choose a finite K≥0 with |e_t(x,τ)|≤K for all (x,τ)∈C*×J. Define b(x)=K·1_{C*}(x). Outside C*, e_t=0 by smoothness and constancy of u on the open spatial complement; hence |e_t(x,τ)|≤b(x) for every x and τ∈J. By N001 every compact set is measurable and has finite Lebesgue measure. M008 makes the constant K integrable on C*, and M009 makes b integrable on ℝⁿ. Define F(τ)(x)=e(x,τ) and Fprime(τ)(x)=e_t(x,τ). Smoothness implies strong measurability; N001 gives F(r) integrable and Fprime(r) strongly measurable. The interval (r/2,3r/2) is a neighborhood of r. The pointwise parameter derivative of F is Fprime by smoothness, and the bound by b holds for every x and every τ in this neighborhood. Thus M006 applies and yields differentiability of E at r with E′(r)=∫e_t(x,r)dx. Since r was arbitrary, the output follows.\\\"},{\\\"op\\\":\\\"replace\\\",\\\"path\\\":\\\"/graph/inferences/3/argument/0/produces\\\",\\\"value\\\":[]}]}\"}"}

event: response.output_item.done
data: {"type":"response.output_item.done","sequence_number":482,"output_index":1,"item":{"type":"function_call","id":"fc_04de6b92baab60ea016adb473600b48191b5dc6c3125f68895","call_id":"call_72a98b231cfa42eb8a94990e7cc595f0","name":"check_artifact","arguments":"{\"artifact_json\":\"{\\\"base_candidate\\\":41,\\\"patches\\\":[{\\\"op\\\":\\\"replace\\\",\\\"path\\\":\\\"/graph/inferences/3/locals/4/from_inputs\\\",\\\"value\\\":[\\\"M019\\\"]},{\\\"op\\\":\\\"replace\\\",\\\"path\\\":\\\"/graph/inferences/3/locals/4/from_local\\\",\\\"value\\\":[]},{\\\"op\\\":\\\"replace\\\",\\\"path\\\":\\\"/graph/inferences/3/argument/0/text\\\",\\\"value\\\":\\\"Fix arbitrary r>0, let J=[r/2,3r/2], choose compact C from H003, and define C*=C∪{0}. The set C* is compact and nonempty; outside C* we still have u=q throughout J. The function (x,τ)↦|e_t(x,τ)| is continuous on C*×J by smoothness, and C*×J is compact by M005. Apply M019; its bounded-above conclusion supplies a real upper bound, so choose a finite K≥0 with |e_t(x,τ)|≤K for all (x,τ)∈C*×J. Define b(x)=K·1_{C*}(x). Outside C*, e_t=0 by smoothness and constancy of u on the open spatial complement; hence |e_t(x,τ)|≤b(x) for every x and τ∈J. By N001 every compact set is measurable and has finite Lebesgue measure. M008 makes the constant K integrable on C*, and M009 makes b integrable on ℝⁿ. Define F(τ)(x)=e(x,τ) and Fprime(τ)(x)=e_t(x,τ). Smoothness implies strong measurability; N001 gives F(r) integrable and Fprime(r) strongly measurable. The interval (r/2,3r/2) is a neighborhood of r. The pointwise parameter derivative of F is Fprime by smoothness, and the bound by b holds for every x and every τ in this neighborhood. Thus M006 applies and yields differentiability of E at r with E′(r)=∫e_t(x,r)dx. Since r was arbitrary, the output follows.\\\"},{\\\"op\\\":\\\"replace\\\",\\\"path\\\":\\\"/graph/inferences/3/argument/0/produces\\\",\\\"value\\\":[]}]}\"}","status":"completed"}}

